      "subsections": {
        "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS",
          "canonical_heading": "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Other causes of hemolytic anemia, including immune-mediated hemolytic anemia (IMHA), babesiosis (not in cats in the United States), cytauxzoonosis (cats only), Heinz body hemolytic anemia (new methylene blue can be used to identify Heinz bodies), micro angiopathic hemolytic anemia, pyruvate kinase deficiency, and phosphofructokinase deficiency (dogs only).",
              "pdf_page": 689,
              "printed_page": 607,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Differentiated from IMHA only by recognition of parasites in blood (stained blood film or species-specific PCR-based assays);",
              "pdf_page": 689,
              "printed_page": 607,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "subheading",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "both disorders (IMHA and/orM. haemofelis",
              "pdf_page": 689,
              "printed_page": 607,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "infection) may be Coombs’ test positive.",
              "pdf_page": 689,
              "printed_page": 607,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "both disorders (IMHA and/orM. haemofelis"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "BabesiaandCytauxzoonspecies are protozoal",
              "pdf_page": 689,
              "printed_page": 607,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "both disorders (IMHA and/orM. haemofelis"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "organisms that differ in morphology from",
              "pdf_page": 689,
              "printed_page": 607,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "both disorders (IMHA and/orM. haemofelis"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "mycoplasma.",
              "pdf_page": 689,
              "printed_page": 607,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "both disorders (IMHA and/orM. haemofelis"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Enzyme assays or specialized DNA tests can",
              "pdf_page": 689,
              "printed_page": 607,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "both disorders (IMHA and/orM. haemofelis"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "be used to diagnose pyruvate kinase and",
              "pdf_page": 689,
              "printed_page": 607,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "both disorders (IMHA and/orM. haemofelis"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "phosphofructokinase deficiencies.",
              "pdf_page": 689,
              "printed_page": 607,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "both disorders (IMHA and/orM. haemofelis"
            }
          ]
        },
        "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS",
          "canonical_heading": "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Anemia most often present with reticulocytosis in animals with clinically important infections—may appear poorly regenerative if precipitous decrease in packed cell volume (PCV) has occurred early in the disease or if there are other concurrent disorders (e.g., FeLV or feline immunodeficiency virus [FIV] infection in cats).",
              "pdf_page": 689,
              "printed_page": 607,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Autoagglutination may be seen in feline blood samples after they cool to below body temperature.",
              "pdf_page": 689,
              "printed_page": 607,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hyperbilirubinemia may be measured and/or seen clinically at times but is seldom severe.",
              "pdf_page": 689,
              "printed_page": 607,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Substantial bilirubinuria seen in some dogs.",
              "pdf_page": 689,
              "printed_page": 607,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Abnormalities related to anemic hypoxia may be shown by clinical chemistry profiles, but profile can be normal.",
              "pdf_page": 689,
              "printed_page": 607,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hypoglycemia possible in moribund cats or if slow to separate blood cells from plasma or serum.",
              "pdf_page": 689,
              "printed_page": 607,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "CBC/CHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS": {
          "present": false,
